// Borrow and Give lend a writer its buffer, as encoding/json keeps its
// encodeState in a pool: a writer that builds a text in a []byte borrows
// an empty buffer, appends to it, takes its text with FromBytes, and gives
// the buffer back for the next writer. Any module's writer may borrow
// (std json's Encode is one, a module's own codec another), so a text
// encoded again allocates its result and no buffer, whoever wrote the
// codec. A buffer that grew out of the pool's size is not kept.

// Borrow is an empty buffer from the pool.
extern go ergo.local/prototype/runtime/v1.StringsBorrow func Borrow() []byte

// Give puts out's buffer back in the pool and gives out, so a writer
// gives its buffer back in place, `t.out = strings.Give(t.out)`: nothing
// may read or write out after.
extern go ergo.local/prototype/runtime/v1.StringsGive func Give(out []byte) []byte

// FromBytes is the text of b, a copy of its bytes.
extern go ergo.local/prototype/runtime/v1.StringsFromBytes func FromBytes(b []byte) string
